          ],
          "content": "4. Take two carbon or metal electrodes and insert them into a piece of cardboard by drilling two holes."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.413,
            0.139,
            0.888,
            0.174
          ],
          "content": "5. Connect one end of each electrode to the terminals of the 9 V battery and to the light bulb."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.413,
            0.174,
            0.888,
            0.226
          ],
          "content": "6. Test the electrical conductivity of each solid sample and observe whether the bulb glows. Record your observations."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.413,
            0.226,
            0.89,
            0.294
          ],
          "content": "7. Test the electrical conductivity of each sample dissolved in water by transferring the solution to the beaker one at a time and observing whether the bulb glows (Fig. 9.15)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.413,
            0.295,
            0.888,
            0.33
          ],
          "content": "8. Record your observations with other samples given in Table 9.2."
        },
